  "app": {
    "title": "FuzzyMind: build, simulate and learn fuzzy cognitive maps",
    "summary": "A person builds a fuzzy cognitive map, a set of concepts joined by signed causal weights. They can type the weights into a matrix, upload a matrix file, write each link as a word such as -High or +Low and convert the words to numbers, or merge maps from several experts. They view the map as a graph, set starting values for the concepts, and run inference that repeats the update until the values settle. A separate page learns the weights from data instead: the person uploads a table, cleans, encodes and normalizes it, picks input and output columns, trains a Neural-FCM model, and then inspects test metrics and the learned weight matrix for any single test instance.",
    "task": {
      "text": "Build a causal map of concepts, from expert judgement or from data, and simulate how the concepts' values change from a starting state until they settle.",
      "verbs": [
        "derive",
        "explore",
        "clean",
        "reconcile"
      ]
    },
    "data": {
      "text": "A square matrix of causal weights between named concepts. The weights are numbers in [-1, 1] or words from a fuzzy term scale with membership functions. On the learning page, the data is a table of variables, with optional rows over time, used to train the weights.",
      "item": "a directed, weighted causal link between two concepts (a cell in the weight matrix)",
      "shapes": [
        "network",
        "table",
        "categories",
        "judgements",
        "time series"
      ]
    },
    "stage": {
      "text": "building and simulating a causal model of a system, from expert judgement or prepared data, to explore scenarios",
      "terms": [
        "analyze",
        "clean"
      ]
    },
    "mechanism": {
      "text": "The causal weights are edited in an editable matrix table, with a graph view of the same matrix that can be toggled on. An inference tab then iterates a starting state vector and shows each step in a table and a line chart. On the learning page, tabs unlock in order: upload, plot, clean, transform, normalize, split, train. Training results show metrics, a picker for one test instance and that instance's weight matrix as a heatmap, plus mean and standard deviation across the test set.",
      "views": [
        "table view",
        "network diagram",
        "line chart",
        "heatmap",
        "form",
        "summary stats"
      ],
      "interactions": [
        "inline edit",
        "import",
        "export",
        "apply label",
        "toggle view",
        "select"
      ],
      "patterns": [
        "category palette",
        "overview and detail"
      ]
    },
    "field": {
      "text": "fuzzy cognitive map modelling and learning in soft computing",
      "disciplines": [
        "computer science"
      ],
      "terms": [
        "fuzzy cognitive maps",
        "fuzzy logic",
        "neural fcm"
      ]
    },
    "workflow": {
      "name": "build a weighted causal map of concepts and simulate it until it settles",
      "steps": [
        {
          "step": "bring the data in",
          "here": "Either set a number of concepts (3–50) and rename them in a one-row table, or upload a weight-matrix CSV with delimiter, decimal and index options. Uploaded matrices can optionally be edited.",
          "parts": [
            "fcm-design",
            "weight-matrix-editor"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "select a stretch and label it with a category",
          "here": "Each matrix cell gets a causal weight in [-1, 1], or a word from a 5-, 7- or 11-term scale (-High … None … +High). The word's triangular, trapezoidal or Gaussian membership function can be reshaped with sliders and downloaded as JSON.",
          "parts": [
            "weight-matrix-editor",
            "linguistic-terms"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "find where people disagree and resolve it",
          "here": "Paired CSV and JSON files from several experts are matched by filename. A table lists every term each expert gave per link, and those terms are combined by fuzzy union. The person can inspect any concept pair's curves before defuzzifying.",
          "parts": [
            "expert-aggregation"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "compute a value from the data",
          "here": "Linguistic or merged weights are defuzzified to numbers by centroid, bisector or mean of maximum.",
          "parts": [
            "linguistic-terms",
            "expert-aggregation"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "look over every item at a glance",
          "here": "A toggle draws the matrix as a directed graph, with edges coloured by sign and weight. Layout, figure size and node size are adjustable, and the figure downloads as PNG.",
          "parts": [
            "fcm-graph"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "compute a value from the data",
          "here": "Choose the iteration cap, equilibrium threshold, update rule (Kosko, Modified Kosko, Rescaled) and activation function (sigmoid with λ and b, tanh, bivalent, trivalent). Enter the initial state vector and press Inference to see each iteration as a table and a line chart.",
          "parts": [
            "inference"
          ]
        },
        {
          "step": "exclude bad items and recompute",
          "here": "Alternatively, learn the weights from data. Upload a CSV, impute missing values or drop them, replace or discard z-score outliers, encode text columns, apply min-max normalization and split inputs from outputs. Then fit Neural-FCM and read the metrics and the per-instance learned weight matrix as a heatmap.",
          "parts": [
            "data-preparation",
            "learning-results"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "distinctive": [
        "Causal links can be given as words on a symmetric negative-to-positive scale. Each word is backed by an editable membership function and turned into a number by defuzzification.",
        "Maps from several experts are merged link by link. The interface shows which term each expert gave and the merged membership curve for any concept pair.",
        "Inference repeats the concept-state update under a selectable rule and activation function. It stops when no concept changes by more than the equilibrium threshold, or at the iteration cap.",
        "Neural-FCM predicts a separate weight matrix for every instance. The results show one test instance's matrix (optionally with the diagonal and output rows zeroed), or the mean and standard deviation across the test set.",
        "Concept values must lie in [0, 1], so only min-max normalization is allowed. A timestep split turns a time series into state-at-t versus state-at-t+k pairs."
      ]
    }
  },
